1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Here is our interoperability playground. Let’s issue a credential,
verify it, and then test a broken signature.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
I choose the standalone mock issuer and verifier, with the reference
wallet as holder.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Starting the flow creates a run ID and executes issuance,
presentation and verification.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:34,000
The control returned Valid. I play the protocol sequence to follow
each component’s messages.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
I open an exchange to inspect its sanitized request and response.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Now I select Bad signature and start another run.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
The verifier returned Invalid. The test passed because rejection was
expected.

8
00:00:51,000 --> 00:01:01,000
I select the two run IDs and compare their configuration, steps and
outcomes.

9
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:08,000
The matrix brings results across combinations into one view.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
The real-wallet catalog contains recorded observations with their
own provenance.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The findings explorer links observed behavior to its evidence and
versions.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:27,000
The party registry describes supported roles, formats and flows.

13
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The capabilities page shows QR payload measurements and support
boundaries.

14
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The Android guide explains the recorded phone journey and network
requirements.

15
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Run pnpm demo:docker and open localhost:5174 to reproduce the mock
prototype.

16
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Now I demonstrate the assisted JSON-LD workflow using fixture data.

17
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,000
I start a connected fixture run. The backend tracks each checkpoint.

18
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:14,000
I simulate issuance, then confirm storage as a separate checkpoint.

19
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
I submit the fixture presentation. The verifier result becomes
Valid.

20
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:27,000
I reload the saved run URL. The fixture outcome remains available.

21
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Here is the recorded local Inji Web card after reload.

22
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Certify issued the JSON-LD credential. The same wallet presented it
to local Verify.

23
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
This setup used synthetic authorization and an explicit DID adapter.

24
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,000
The managed inspector links captured exchanges to the local run.

25
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Permitted field names are shown while credential values are
withheld.

26
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Browser traffic and Mimoto server calls have distinct capture
coverage.

27
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The hosted Web journey has its own recorded run and valid
verification.

28
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Its environment and timestamps remain separate from the local
journey.

29
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,000
This physical Android record used local Certify and hosted Verify.

30
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:33,000
The operator stored and reopened the card, then presented it
successfully.

31
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:39,000
A managed all-local Android run remains open.

32
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Here we compare issuer key handling across two Verify versions.

33
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:53,000
ES256 and RS256 passed with a matching certificate and failed
without one.

34
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:01,000
The native walt.id credential also passed when supplied with its
signer’s certificate.

35
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Our claims table links each result to saved evidence and its limits.

36
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
It covers authorization code with PKCE, pre-authorized issuance and
cross-vendor discovery adapters.

37
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:22,000
It also records the mDL integrity checks, browser credential chooser
and QR measurements.

38
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,000
The matrix indexes 79 observations and the register tracks 15
findings.

39
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:35,000
The sample-data index gives synthetic inputs, instructions and
expected outcomes.

40
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:46,000
The API reference documents the backend. Test reports record the
checks actually executed.

41
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
The readiness page records completed checks and remaining human
tasks.

42
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Reviewers can run the Docker prototype or inspect the public
evidence archive.
